{
 "_doc": "The tine FE eigenproblem (tine_fe: 78 dofs, K and M each conditioned ~1e9) is ill-conditioned: scipy eigh gives the partials to only ~1e-7 relative, and the last bits depend on the BLAS / LAPACK build (Accelerate on macOS, OpenBLAS on Linux). tune_spring's 60-step bisection, the hammer contacts and the integer rounding downstream turn those bits into different header words (533 values on arm64 Linux). So gen_rhodes.py reads each key's FE results (spring mass, the 6 partials, the mode shapes at the strike point and the tip) from this file, written on the reference Mac, and every machine builds the same rhodes_tables.h. Everything after the FE (the 2-DOF tine-bar, the hammer, the golden model's calibration) then gives the same integer words, measured on macOS arm64, Linux arm64 and Linux x86_64 (only rhodes_spec.json's floats may differ in the last ulps).",
